From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: W4HO <87742609+W4HO-ham@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 21:52:18 -0644
Subject: [PATCH] rtc: s5m: wait for the S2MPG14's read update too

The S2MPG14 has the S2MPG10's RTC registers, and the same read update:
the time registers get a copy of the counters on a rising edge of RUDR,
and the copy takes time. Commit 210e28ff7eaa ("rtc: s5m: wait for the
S2MPG10's read update before reading the time") clears RUDR, waits, sets
it and waits again on the S2MPG10. Commit 58544d878d8a ("rtc: s5m: wait
for the S2MPG12's read update too") does the same on the S2MPG12. The
S2MPG14 still reads the registers before the copy lands and can get the
time of the read before.

The S2MPG14 is the main PMIC of three boards here, the Pixel 9a
(zumapro-tegu), the Pixel 8 Pro (zuma-husky) and the Pixel 8a
(zuma-akita), so the RTC read changes on all three. It then matches
Google's driver for this PMIC on all of them: s2m_rtc_update() in
rtc-s2mpg14.c clears the read bit, waits 50 us, sets it and waits 1 ms
before every read, and the file is the same on the Pixel 9a's and the
Pixel 8's kernel branches. This was run on the 9a only.

On a Pixel 9a, 60 rounds of two reads of
/sys/class/rtc/rtc0/since_epoch after a 20 s pause, each compared with
the system clock: the first read was 20 s behind in two rounds on this
tree, and on an older build of the same driver in four, with one more
10 s behind. The second read, a moment later, was right every time.
2,500 reads 5 to 90 ms apart showed none off, the read before being
that recent. In 310 suspends with a read before and after each, five of
the differences were off while the kernel's clock had every sleep at
60 s: three by 20 or 21 s and one by 7 s, which is what an old read
gives, and one by 39 s, which fits a read that mixed the old time with
the new and has not been reproduced awake.

rtcwake sets its alarm relative to its read, so a read that old moves
the alarm back by its age, and one older than the interval asked for
puts the alarm in the past.

Take the S2MPG10's path on the S2MPG14 too. With it, the same 60 rounds
returned no old time; at two in 60 before, that alone would happen by
chance about one time in eight. The build with this change has been
through 10 suspends of 60 s so far, each woken by the RTC alarm with the
two reads 62 or 63 s apart, as a 60 s sleep gives.

Signed-off-by: W4HO <87742609+W4HO-ham@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/rtc/rtc-s5m.c | 9 ++++++---
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/rtc/rtc-s5m.c b/drivers/rtc/rtc-s5m.c
index dde6d2b81..1e997bf8c 100644
--- a/drivers/rtc/rtc-s5m.c
+++ b/drivers/rtc/rtc-s5m.c
@@ -357,10 +357,12 @@ static int s5m_rtc_read_time(struct device *dev, struct rtc_time *tm)
 		 * read at once, a read could return a time seconds old, and
 		 * an alarm set from it fired early or not at all. Clear it
 		 * first and wait for the copy, as Google's s2mpg10 driver does.
-		 * The S2MPG12's RTC has the same registers and the same copy.
+		 * The S2MPG12's and the S2MPG14's RTCs have the same registers
+		 * and the same copy.
 		 */
 		if (info->device_type == S2MPG10 ||
-		    info->device_type == S2MPG12) {
+		    info->device_type == S2MPG12 ||
+		    info->device_type == S2MPG14) {
 			ret = regmap_clear_bits(info->regmap, info->regs->udr_update,
 						info->regs->read_time_udr_mask);
 			if (ret)
@@ -372,7 +374,8 @@ static int s5m_rtc_read_time(struct device *dev, struct rtc_time *tm)
 		if (ret)
 			goto udr_fail;
 		if (info->device_type == S2MPG10 ||
-		    info->device_type == S2MPG12)
+		    info->device_type == S2MPG12 ||
+		    info->device_type == S2MPG14)
 			usleep_range(1000, 1100);
 	}
 	ret = regmap_bulk_read(info->regmap, info->regs->time, data,
